# API Design for TypeScript

Extends `core/rules/api-design.mdc`. Runtime concerns (process lifecycle, config, logging) live in the Node rules; type-level patterns live in `typescript.mdc`.

## Framework choice

| Framework | Use when |
|-----------|----------|
| Express 5 | Simple services, large middleware ecosystem. Rejected promises from async handlers are forwarded to the error handler natively. |
| Fastify | Throughput matters; built-in JSON Schema validation and serialisation (use `fastify-type-provider-zod` for Zod). |
| NestJS | Large teams wanting opinionated modules, DI, and decorators. |

Examples below target **Express 5**. On Express 4, wrap async handlers (`(fn) => (req, res, next) => fn(req, res, next).catch(next)`) or use `express-async-errors`.

## Thin handlers

Handlers parse input, call one service/use case, and map the result. No business logic, no SQL.

```typescript
router.get('/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const { id } = IdParams.parse(req.params);
  const user = await userService.getUser(id);   // throws NotFoundError
  res.json(toUserResponse(user));               // map domain -> DTO; never leak entities
});
```

## Validate every input with Zod

Validate `params`, `query`, and `body` at the boundary; derive types from schemas.

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const IdParams = z.object({ id: z.coerce.number().int().positive() });
const CreateUserBody = z.object({
  email: z.email().max(255),
  name: z.string().min(2).max(100),
});
type CreateUserBody = z.infer<typeof CreateUserBody>;

const validateBody = <T extends z.ZodType>(schema: T): RequestHandler => (req, _res, next) => {
  const result = schema.safeParse(req.body);
  if (!result.success) return next(new ValidationError(z.flattenError(result.error).fieldErrors));
  req.body = result.data;
  next();
};

router.post('/users', validateBody(CreateUserBody), async (req, res) => {
  const user = await userService.create(req.body as CreateUserBody);
  res.status(201).location(`/users/${user.id}`).json(toUserResponse(user));
});
```

## Typed errors and one error handler

```typescript
export class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(readonly status: number, message: string, readonly code: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = new.target.name;
  }
}
export class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  constructor(resource: string) { super(404, `${resource} not found`, 'not_found'); }
}
export class ValidationError extends AppError {
  constructor(readonly errors: Record<string, string[] | undefined>) {
    super(400, 'Validation failed', 'validation_failed');
  }
}

// Register last. Responds with RFC 9457 Problem Details.
export const errorHandler: ErrorRequestHandler = (err, req, res, _next) => {
  if (err instanceof AppError) {
    res.status(err.status).type('application/problem+json').json({
      type: `about:blank`, title: err.message, status: err.status, code: err.code,
      ...(err instanceof ValidationError && { errors: err.errors }),
    });
    return;
  }
  req.log?.error({ err }, 'unhandled error');
  res.status(500).type('application/problem+json').json({ title: 'Internal Server Error', status: 500 });
};
```

- Never send stack traces or internal messages for 5xx responses
- Map domain errors to status codes in one place, not in each handler

## Authentication middleware

```typescript
export const authenticate: RequestHandler = async (req, _res, next) => {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.match(/^Bearer (.+)$/)?.[1];
  if (!token) throw new AppError(401, 'Missing token', 'unauthenticated');
  req.user = await verifyAccessToken(token);   // validates signature, alg, iss, aud, exp
  next();
};
```

Augment `Express.Request` via declaration merging (`declare global { namespace Express { interface Request { user?: AuthUser } } }`) instead of casting.

## Responses

- Plural nouns, no verbs in paths: `GET /orders`, `POST /orders`, `GET /orders/{id}`
- `201` + `Location` for creates, `204` for deletes, `202` for accepted async work
- Paginate collections: `{ data, page: { cursor | page, limit, total? } }`; cap `limit`
- Do not wrap every success in `{ status: 'success' }`; HTTP status already carries it

## OpenAPI

Generate the spec from the same schemas used for validation so they cannot drift:

- Zod: `@asteasolutions/zod-to-openapi` or `zod-openapi`
- Fastify: `@fastify/swagger` from route schemas
- NestJS: `@nestjs/swagger` decorators / CLI plugin

Serve the document in non-production environments or publish it as a build artefact.

## Rate limiting and CORS

```typescript
import rateLimit from 'express-rate-limit';
import cors from 'cors';

app.use('/api/', rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60_000, limit: 100, standardHeaders: 'draft-8', legacyHeaders: false }));
app.post('/api/auth/login', rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60_000, limit: 5 }), loginHandler);

app.use(cors({ origin: config.CORS_ORIGINS, credentials: true }));   // explicit allow-list; never '*' with credentials
```

Use a shared store (e.g. Redis) for rate limits when running more than one instance.

## Checklist

- [ ] Params, query, and body validated with Zod; types inferred from schemas
- [ ] Handlers are thin; domain entities mapped to response DTOs
- [ ] Typed error classes; single error handler returning Problem Details
- [ ] Express 5 (or async wrapper on Express 4) so async errors reach the handler
- [ ] OpenAPI generated from validation schemas
- [ ] Rate limiting on public and auth endpoints; CORS allow-list from config
